i've recently come across multiple 500  year old maps that are so interesting  yet so bizarre i cannot understand how  i've never seen or heard of these before  and i suspect by the end of this video  you'll likely feel the same because what  these maps show in the middle of the  sahara desert  is just not supposed to be there  now let me first primer your brain into  what you're about to see by sharing one  of the more unusual yet fascinating  discoveries made in just the last few  years in fact something that surprises  pretty much everyone when they hear it  for the first time which is the fact  that the sahara desert was a lush green  tropical paradise as recently as just 5  000 years ago in fact some sources  estimating as little as 4 500 years ago  before it rapidly transitioned to the  vast hot desert that we're all so  familiar with today consisting of  endless mountains of sand dunes that  span further than the eyes can see  a truly remarkable discovery considering  we had presumed for so long that the  sahara must surely have been millions of  years old and get this  not only was it a lush wetland full of  vegetation but it also had a massive  network of rivers in fact one of which  would rank among the world's largest  today but perhaps even more interesting  than that is the fact that in the middle  of the sahara desert was the world's  largest freshwater lake ever known to  have existed  so large in fact that this one lake had  50 percent more surface area than all  the north american great lakes combined  and all of this the rivers the lakes and  lush vegetation that made up the green  sahara disappeared in almost an instant  just a few thousand years ago  wild right but these figures of 4 500 or  5 000 years ago you see that's the part  where things start to get strange  for example take this little-known yet  incredible map from louis ii of france  which dates back to the year 1551.  there are so many aspects of this map  that are worthy of study and discussion  however let's focus a moment on the  sahara desert and notice that there are  inlets of water streaming into the  african continent from the north the  west and the south all along the  coastline  something that no longer exists today  but also notice the river that is  flowing into the middle of the sahara  what river is this supposed to be  exactly because surely it should not be  confused with the single river or the  senegal river excuse me which travels  east southeast into africa but nowhere  near as far east as this river is shown  to reach particularly as it's annotated  on other maps  take this one for example which is  another little-known map and was found  in atlas created by portuguese  cartographer diego hamem and also dates  to the mid 1500s and marks the same  massive river flowing all the way clear  through the middle of the desert into  the eastern sahara nearly reaching the  nile river and again let's not forget  the key detail here which is that this  is from nearly 500 years ago  which i mean come on where is this river  today and why have i never heard of it  before  and by the way when i point out that it  nearly reaches the nile river let's take  a look at yet another map and this one  was created in the year 1578  by gerard mercader certainly a name that  will ring a bell to far more people  however i'll venture to say that most  people are still unlikely to have ever  seen this before much less studied the  details annotated on this map which like  i mentioned a moment ago illustrates a  diverse river traveling through the  middle of the entire sahara until it  nearly reaches the nile  and by the way notice how it shows the  nile river extensively branching off in  various directions that also no longer  exist today i find that quite  interesting  but let me now point your attention to  the northern sahara and observe the  multiple rivers that seemingly flow  either into or from the mediterranean  sea  and notice that illustrates various  tribes cities or settlements that are  shown to have existed all along these  rivers and waterways  and while we're at it take a look at  another map this one is from 1645  created by petrus bertius that is based  on that same arcada map you just saw  now this image is obviously a bit blurry  but it still paints us a picture into  the nature of these mysterious river  systems but that's not all  take a look at this spectacular map that  was first published in the year 1639  by a claes johnson visher  certainly the image is also a bit blurry  like the other one when cropped but if  you look closely you can still see a  vast network of rivers traveling in  virtually every direction throughout the  sahara you know when i see these various  maps i can't help but be reminded of the  legends that claim that the nile river  once flowed from east to west because  let's look at this map here that was  created some 400 years ago in the early  1600s following the voyage of willem  shouten in the year 1619 after he was  the first to sail around cape horn  but notice how it shows the nile river  going further south again into  sub-saharan africa before it then turns  abruptly west  tell me that's not interesting because  it keeps bringing me back to the same  question which is  why have i never seen or heard of these  maps before and not only that why do  they show these particular or  spectacular rivers i should say in the  middle of the sahara desert as recently  as just four and five hundred years ago  a key detail that cannot be overstated  i mean since when is any of this  supposed to exist in the middle of the  sahara at that period of time and make  no mistake these rivers would certainly  have been an oasis for ancient human  civilizations that would have  undoubtedly occupied the lands around  them  i mean it's worth mentioning that there  are recent scientific studies that have  concluded exactly that which when you  think about it makes all the sense in  the world as human migration has long  been known to travel along rivers as  well as along coastlines where  settlements and entire civilizations are  then established  i've said it before and i'll say it  again the sahara desert has got to be  the hidden jackpot for lost ancient  human civilizations yet it receives  arguably the least amount of attention  not just from history enthusiasts but  from the archaeological community as  well in fact it's been said that much of  the sahara particularly the western  sahara remains literally and  figuratively almost blank as far as the  wider international research community  is concerned which i have to say is  incredibly unfortunate as evidence has  shown the existence of civilizations in  this region dating back more than 11 and  a half thousand years ago  so the question becomes  what is waiting to be discovered below  the endless sea of sand which could  potentially  be a preserve for archaeological  evidence dating back thousands of years  or perhaps far more recently as these  various century old maps seem to  indicate  honestly isn't it a bit weird that  you've never seen or heard of these maps  before i mean certainly there are some  people watching this that are familiar  with some of them but let's be real it's  not taught in school and without a doubt  a vast overwhelming percentage of the  masses have never seen or heard of these  before but to make things even more  bizarre than that these are just a few  of the ones that i've recently come  across in fact the most interesting map  of them all is 2 000 years old and it  shows something in the sahara desert  that is related to atlantis and that is  going to be the topic of my next video  in fact this video was going to include  all the above all these maps and the  topic of atlantis but there's so much to  unpack that i had to divide it into two  videos and i'll be honest and i know  people hate hype but that next video is  going to be quite interesting now that  said leave a comment and share what your  thoughts are on these maps that again  they show rivers and all kinds of things  happening in the sahara desert four and  five hundred years ago  that's not supposed to be there and  honestly it raises significant questions  and by the way if you're wondering why  you've never seen a herd of these before  it's because they sit in basements in  museums and never see the light of day  it's it's a tragedy actually so give  this video a thumbs up if you've never  seen or heard of these before or if you  found the content interesting and again  leave a a comment and share your  thoughts but i'm jimmy corsetti my  channel is called bright insight stay  tuned to my next video like i said is  going to be talking about atlantis  involving the sahara desert which i know  many people will find quite interesting  and also follow me on instagram and  support me and my channel on patreon  subscribe star or locals i'll have the  links down below but all of that said  i'll close it up again i'm jimmy my  channel is called bright insight and  stay tuned to another interesting video  real soon take care everybody
 

